#!/usr/bin/env -S python3 -u
"""
arc-standup-notify: hand the standup prep over fifteen minutes before the standup.

The eight lines this delivers have been correct for weeks and were still not being used,
because correct is not the same as present. They sit on a page that has to be opened, and
the quarter hour before a Monday, Wednesday or Friday call is exactly the moment nobody
remembers a page exists -- measured, over two weeks: not one of the page's own controls
was ever touched. So this is the same composition, pushed instead of pulled.

**A program of its own rather than a flag on arcs-refresh.** arcs-refresh is a build
runner, and its whole body -- a quota guard, a lock, a preflight, a pipeline -- is there
because a rebuild spends money and moves a run-over-run baseline. This spends nothing,
builds nothing and takes no lock: it reads one string off the disk and puts it on a
screen. Arriving as a flag would mean inheriting a preflight for programs it never runs
and a guard against a bill it cannot incur. What it does share is the log. `refresh.log`
is where the unattended half of this workstream accounts for itself, and a second file to
forget to look in is worse than one shared file with a prefix on the line.

**Why the morning's text and not a fresh one.** The 07:10 build's composition is three
hours old by 10:15, and re-running the pipeline slice at 10:10 was the alternative. It
loses on three counts, and only the third is interesting.

The dull two: a run costs model calls, so it would have to sit behind the quota guard and
the lock, and the notification's reliability would become the pipeline's -- five minutes
of git, GitLab, Jira and Slack between the timer and the screen, on a laptop that has been
awake for ten minutes. Prep that arrives at 10:22, or not at all because the wifi was
still associating, is worse than prep that arrives at 10:15 slightly short.

The third is that the staleness is not where it looks. arc-standup's window opens at the
*last* standup and closes at the *next* one, so the 07:10 build on a Wednesday and a 10:15
read on the same Wednesday are answering about the same interval: Monday 10:30 to now. The
selection is not stale at all. What the morning's text is missing is what happened between
07:10 and 10:15 -- which is the one part of the last two days still in his head. The prep
is here to recover Friday afternoon, not this morning.

That is also the freshness test, and it is exact rather than a guess at an age: the sidecar
records the standup it was composed for, and it is delivered only when that is the standup
about to happen. A page built any time since Monday's call is prep for Wednesday's. A page
built before Monday's call is prep for a meeting that has been and gone -- and what that
gets is the pointer below, never the wrong eight lines.

The sidecar is `standup.json` beside the page, written by `arcs` at the end of a finished
build. It carries `text` verbatim, which is the field the page's copy button also carries,
so the spoken version, the pasted version and the notified version stay one composition --
arc-standup's rule, and the reason nothing here re-composes anything.

**The clock is checked twice, and nothing catches up.** The timer this installs sets
`Persistent=false`, which is the exact opposite of the refresh timer beside it and for the
exact same reason read backwards: a rebuild missed at 07:10 is worth doing at 08:04 when
the lid opens, and prep missed at 10:15 is worth nothing at 11:00 because the standup has
happened. Skip, do not catch up. But a schedule is a convenience and not the authority --
the cadence lives in `STANDUP_DAYS`/`STANDUP_TIME`/`STANDUP_TZ` and is read here through
arc-standup's own `cadence()`, so a knob changed in .env changes what this does on the
next run whether or not anybody remembered to reinstall the unit. The gate is therefore in
the program: it speaks only in the window that closes when the standup opens, and a unit
that fires at the wrong time produces a logged refusal rather than a late interruption.

**A popup rather than a notification, because of how tall the block is.** dunst caps a
notification's height -- 300px here, about thirteen lines -- and the composed block runs
to twenty-one, so what a notification drops is its tail: BLOCKED / ASKS and IN FRONT OF
YOU, the two beats a person on the call can act on. claude-notify-popup.py sizes its
window to its content, which is what it was written for, so it gets the block and
claude-notify.sh sits behind it for a machine with no GTK or no display. See `surfaces`.

**It gives, and never asks.** No question, no queue, no "did you update the board" -- the
shape being avoided here is a status bot, and a prompt that arrives uninvited is one the
eye learns to skip. At most one notification per standup, recorded in a marker beside the
sidecar, and the pointer below spends that one just as the block does.

**When the block is not there, the moment still is.** A build that never ran, a sidecar
that will not parse, a page not rebuilt since the last call -- in the quarter hour before a
standup each of those is answered with a short card carrying the clock, the sentence naming
which of them happened, and the link. That is the shape the 10:15 popup had before any of
this, kept for exactly the case it was always right for: correct notes are what this
delivers, and knowing there are none is worth more than a silence indistinguishable from
the timer being off. The degradation is on the screen and not only in refresh.log, because
that file is read after something has gone wrong and been noticed, and being noticed is the
part that was missing. The gate order is what keeps this from becoming the status bot: the
clock is asked first, so a half-written state directory on a Tuesday is silent and the same
directory at 10:15 on a Wednesday is not. See `decide`.

The one silence that stays is a block that built and is empty. That is a finished report
rather than a failure, and pointing at a page to say there is nothing on it is the worst
ratio this could send. Every refusal still writes its sentence to refresh.log, because a
job nobody watches has to be able to say what it did.

    arc-standup-notify                  gate on the clock, then notify -- what the timer runs
    arc-standup-notify --check          say what it would do and why, and do nothing
    arc-standup-notify --print          print the prep it holds, to stdout
    arc-standup-notify --force          notify now, whatever the clock says -- and without
                                        spending the morning's one automatic delivery
    arc-standup-notify --at 2026-08-26T10:16   pretend it is that instant
    arc-standup-notify --install-timer  the systemd user timer, derived from the cadence
    arc-standup-notify --uninstall-timer

Tuning, read from .env or the environment:
    STANDUP_NOTIFY_LEAD    minutes before the standup this speaks in       (default 15)
    STANDUP_DAYS/TIME/TZ   arc-standup's cadence, shared and not duplicated
    WORK_ARCS_ARTIFACT_URL the published page, named in the last line of the notification
    WORK_ARCS_NOTIFY       one notifier instead of the two below, taking summary and body
    WORK_ARCS_NOTIFY_MARKUP  set to 1 when that one parses its body as Pango markup
    STANDUP_NOTIFY_ACCENT  the popup's border colour                      (#a8d8a8)

Exit codes are distinct because a unit reads them: 0 delivered -- the block or the
pointer, both of them being something that reached the screen -- 3 decided not to
speak and said why in the log, 1 it should have spoken and the delivery failed, 2 a
bad argument. Only 1 is a failure -- the timer's unit names 3 in SuccessExitStatus,
because "today is not a standup day" sitting in `systemctl --user status` as a red
line is how a status command trains you to stop reading it. A pointer is 0 rather
than its own code for the same reason: it is the degraded mode working, and the log
line saying so is where that belongs.
"""

import argparse
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import os
import json
import re
import subprocess
import sys
from datetime import datetime, timedelta
from pathlib import Path

HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
REPO = HERE.parent

STATE = (Path(os.environ.get("XDG_STATE_HOME", Path.home() / ".local/state"))
         / "work-arcs")
SIDECAR = STATE / "standup.json"
MARKER = STATE / "standup-notified.json"
LOG_FILE = STATE / "refresh.log"

UNIT_DIR = (Path(os.environ.get("XDG_CONFIG_HOME", Path.home() / ".config"))
            / "systemd" / "user")
SERVICE_UNIT = "work-arcs-standup.service"
TIMER_UNIT = "work-arcs-standup.timer"

SYSTEMD_DAYS = ["Mon", "Tue", "Wed", "Thu", "Fri", "Sat", "Sun"]


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- the environment
#
# Read here rather than left to the unit, and the reason is the same one the arcs-refresh
# header spends four paragraphs on: a systemd user service gets an environment nobody
# tested against, and the cadence knobs live in .env. `EnvironmentFile=` would half-work --
# it does not know about `export`, quotes or anything else a shell file may carry -- so the
# parse is here, where the same call serves a timer, a cron line and a person typing the
# name. Anything already set wins: an override on the command line is an override.

def load_env(path=None):
    path = Path(path or REPO / ".env")
    if not path.is_file():
        return
    for raw in path.read_text(errors="replace").splitlines():
        line = raw.strip()
        if not line or line.startswith("#"):
            continue
        if line.startswith("export "):
            line = line[len("export "):].strip()
        m = re.match(r"^([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)=(.*)$", line)
        if not m:
            continue
        key, val = m.group(1), m.group(2).strip()
        if len(val) >= 2 and val[0] == val[-1] and val[0] in "\"'":
            val = val[1:-1]
        os.environ.setdefault(key, val)


def arc_standup():
    """arc-standup as a module, for its cadence and nothing else.

    Imported rather than shelled out to because the question being asked -- when is the
    next standup, in which timezone, with which fallbacks for a typo in STANDUP_DAYS -- is
    already answered there in sixty lines that are already tested. A second implementation
    of a calendar is a second calendar, and the two disagree the first time daylight
    saving moves.
    """
    src = HERE / "arc-standup"
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("arc_standup", str(src))
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader("arc_standup", loader)
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    loader.exec_module(mod)
    return mod


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- saying what happened

def log(msg):
    """One line in the refresh log, prefixed so the two programs sharing it stay legible."""
    stamp = datetime.now().astimezone().strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S%z")
    try:
        STATE.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        with LOG_FILE.open("a") as fh:
            fh.write(f"{stamp}  standup-notify: {msg}\n")
    except OSError:
        pass
    if sys.stdout.isatty():
        print(msg)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- the gate
#
# Every refusal is a sentence rather than a boolean, because the only witness to a morning
# this said nothing on is the log, and "no" is not a diagnosis. The order is the order a
# person would ask in: is there prep, is it for this meeting, does it say anything, is it
# time, and have I already handed it over.

def read_sidecar():
    """The prep, and -- when there is none -- which kind of nothing it is.

    Told apart because the two nothings read differently at 10:15 and are fixed by
    different things: a file that was never written means no build has finished since the
    last standup, and a file that will not parse means one finished and wrote something
    broken. Both degrade to the same pointer, and the person reading it needs the sentence.
    """
    try:
        raw = SIDECAR.read_text()
    except OSError:
        return None, f"nothing has built {SIDECAR} since the last standup"
    try:
        data = json.loads(raw)
    except ValueError:
        return None, f"the prep at {SIDECAR} will not parse"
    if not isinstance(data, dict):
        return None, f"the prep at {SIDECAR} is not the shape a finished build writes"
    return data, None


def read_marker():
    try:
        data = json.loads(MARKER.read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}
    return data if isinstance(data, dict) else {}


def write_marker(target_iso, now):
    try:
        STATE.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        MARKER.write_text(json.dumps(
            {"v": 1, "for": target_iso, "at": now.isoformat()}, indent=1) + "\n")
    except OSError:
        pass


def fmt_minutes(mins):
    if mins < 90:
        return f"{int(round(mins))} min"
    hours = mins / 60
    if hours < 36:
        return f"{hours:.0f}h"
    return f"{hours / 24:.0f}d"


def decide(cad, st, missing_why, now, lead, force=False):
    """What to say, and the sentence that says why.

    Returns (mode, why, overrode). `mode` is "full" when the composed block is ready to
    hand over, "pointer" when the moment has come and the block has not, and "silent" when
    this is not the moment. `overrode` names the gates --force walked past, so a forced
    delivery cannot be mistaken in the log for one the clock asked for.

    **The clock is asked before the prep, and that order is the whole of the degradation.**
    A page that failed to build is worth interrupting somebody about only in the quarter
    hour they were going to be interrupted anyway. Asked the other way round -- prep first,
    the order this had when silence was its only refusal -- every afternoon with a
    half-written state directory becomes a notification, which is the status bot this is
    written not to be.

    Every refusal is still a sentence rather than a boolean, because the only witness to a
    morning this said nothing on is the log, and "no" is not a diagnosis.
    """
    overrode = []

    target = (cad.get("for") or {}).get("iso")
    if not target:
        return "silent", "the cadence names no upcoming standup", overrode
    try:
        when = datetime.fromisoformat(target)
    except ValueError:
        return "silent", f"the cadence gave an unreadable instant ({target!r})", overrode
    minutes = (when - now).total_seconds() / 60.0

    # Open on one side only. Below zero cannot happen -- arc-standup's `for` is the next
    # standup at or after `now` -- but the standup minute itself is deliberately outside:
    # at 10:30 the call is starting and an interruption is no longer prep.
    if not (0 < minutes <= lead):
        day = (cad.get("for") or {}).get("day", "the next standup")
        why = (f"{day}'s standup is {fmt_minutes(minutes)} away — this speaks in the "
               f"{lead} minutes before one, and never after it has started")
        if not force:
            return "silent", why, overrode
        overrode.append("the clock")

    seen = read_marker().get("for")
    if seen == target:
        why = f"already delivered for {(cad.get('for') or {}).get('day', 'this standup')}"
        if not force:
            return "silent", why, overrode
        overrode.append("the once-a-standup marker")

    # Past here the moment is real -- a standup morning, inside the window, nobody told yet
    # -- and something reaches the screen unless the block itself is the reason not to.

    if st is None:
        return "pointer", missing_why, overrode

    # The freshness test, and the whole of it: the prep names the meeting it was composed
    # for. Same meeting, right window -- a build from any time since the last standup was
    # computing this exact interval. A different meeting means the page has not been
    # rebuilt since that one happened, and its notes are a record of a call already given.
    # Under --force it is handed over regardless: somebody who typed --force is asking to
    # see what is there, and a pointer is not an answer to that question.
    built_for = (st.get("for") or {}).get("iso")
    if built_for != target:
        was = (st.get("for") or {}).get("when") or "an earlier standup"
        why = (f"the prep on disk is for {was}, which has been and gone — nothing has "
               "rebuilt the page since")
        if not force:
            return "pointer", why, overrode
        overrode.append("staleness")

    # A built block with nothing in it is the one silence that stays, and it is not a
    # degradation: it is a finished report that happens to be empty. Interrupting somebody
    # to point at a page and say there is nothing on it is the worst ratio here.
    if st.get("empty"):
        why = "nothing to report in this window — saying so out loud is the standup's job"
        if not force:
            return "silent", why, overrode
        overrode.append("an empty block")

    return "full", (f"{(cad.get('for') or {}).get('when', 'the standup')} is "
                    f"{fmt_minutes(minutes)} away"), overrode


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- delivery

def lead_phrase(cad, now):
    """How long is left, which is the half of the summary both kinds of card share."""
    head = cad.get("for") or {}
    try:
        mins = (datetime.fromisoformat(head["iso"]) - now).total_seconds() / 60.0
        return f"Standup in {fmt_minutes(mins)}"
    except (KeyError, ValueError):
        return "Standup soon"


def summary_line(cad, now):
    return f"{lead_phrase(cad, now)} — {(cad.get('for') or {}).get('when', 'next')}"


def pointer(cad, now, why):
    """The card for the morning the block is not there: the clock, the reason, the link.

    This is the shape the 10:15 popup had before any of this existed -- a nudge and a URL
    -- and it is kept for exactly the case that shape was always right for. A page that
    failed to build is the morning somebody most needs telling, and telling them in
    refresh.log is telling nobody: the file is read when something has already gone wrong
    and been noticed, which is the state this is trying to prevent.

    It says which failure it hit rather than "unavailable", because the two have different
    repairs -- a build that never ran wants `arcs`, a build that ran and wrote a broken
    sidecar wants looking at -- and a person with fourteen minutes can only act on the one
    they can name. The link is the last line either way, in the same place the full card
    puts it, so the click is in the same place whichever card arrived.
    """
    head = cad.get("for") or {}
    day = (head.get("day") or "the next").upper()
    lines = [f"FOR {day}'S STANDUP — the notes are not there.", "", why + ".", ""]
    url = os.environ.get("WORK_ARCS_ARTIFACT_URL", "").strip()
    lines.append(url if url else
                 "No WORK_ARCS_ARTIFACT_URL to point at either — run arcs.")
    return f"{lead_phrase(cad, now)} — no prep to hand over", "\n".join(lines)


def body_text(st):
    """The block, plus the one line that makes the page reachable from the popup.

    The URL is a line of the body rather than a notification action: both surfaces below
    fire and forget, so there is no process left alive for an action to call back into.
    Inventing a third notifier to gain a button would be a worse trade than the one line
    costs, and dunst offers a URL in a body to the middle click on its own.
    """
    text = (st.get("text") or "").rstrip("\n")
    url = os.environ.get("WORK_ARCS_ARTIFACT_URL", "").strip()
    if url:
        text += f"\n\n{url}"
    return text


def pango_escape(s):
    """Escaped only for a surface that parses its body as Pango markup, which is dunst.

    An ampersand in a ticket subject makes the whole block fail to parse there, and what
    dunst then shows is not the block. The GTK popup takes plain text, and escaping for it
    would put a literal ampersand-a-m-p on the screen -- so this is done per surface on the
    way out rather than once to the body.
    """
    return s.replace("&", "&amp;").replace("<", "&lt;").replace(">", "&gt;")


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- the surface
#
# The popup is not the obvious choice and is the right one, for a reason that is a property
# of this block rather than a preference. dunst's notification height is capped -- 300px on
# this machine, about thirteen lines of its Monospace 16 -- and the composed block runs to
# twenty-one with its beat headers, its blank lines and the URL. What gets cut is the tail,
# which is BLOCKED / ASKS and IN FRONT OF YOU: the two beats holding what a person on the
# call can act on. A notification that reliably drops the actionable half of the prep is
# not a smaller version of this feature.
#
# claude-notify-popup.py sizes its window to its content and has no such cap, which is what
# it was written for. What it gives up is dunst's history and the chime, so the block is
# gone when it times out -- which is why --print exists and hands over the same string.
# claude-notify.sh is kept behind it rather than dropped: on a machine with no GTK or no
# display, a clipped block beats no block, and the fallback says so in the log.
NOTIFY_POPUP = Path.home() / "bin/notification/claude-notify-popup.py"
NOTIFY_SEND = Path.home() / "bin/notification/claude-notify.sh"

# A fixed colour rather than claude-notify.sh's per-session hash: this is the same card
# three mornings a week, and being recognisable before it is read is most of its job.
ACCENT = os.environ.get("STANDUP_NOTIFY_ACCENT", "#a8d8a8")

# How long a surface is given to fail before it counts as delivered. Waiting for the popup
# to *exit* would be waiting for the notification to be over -- it holds its own window for
# fifteen seconds. The only failure either of these has is immediate: no display, no GTK,
# no session bus.
EARLY_FAILURE_SECONDS = 2.5


def surfaces():
    """Where the block is offered, in order, each with whether it parses markup."""
    override = os.environ.get("WORK_ARCS_NOTIFY", "").strip()
    if override:
        return [(Path(override), os.environ.get("WORK_ARCS_NOTIFY_MARKUP", "") == "1")]
    return [(NOTIFY_POPUP, False), (NOTIFY_SEND, True)]


def hand_over(exe, markup, summary, body):
    if not os.access(exe, os.X_OK):
        return False, f"no notifier at {exe}"
    text = pango_escape(body) if markup else body
    try:
        proc = subprocess.Popen([str(exe), summary, text, ACCENT],
                                stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.PIPE)
    except OSError as e:
        return False, f"could not run {exe} ({e})"
    try:
        rc = proc.wait(timeout=EARLY_FAILURE_SECONDS)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return True, ""                      # still up, so it is on the screen
    if rc != 0:
        err = (proc.stderr.read() or b"").decode(errors="replace").strip().splitlines()
        return False, f"{exe.name} exited {rc}" + (f": {err[-1]}" if err else "")
    return True, ""


def deliver(summary, body):
    """The first surface that takes it, and what the ones before it said if any refused."""
    tried = []
    for exe, markup in surfaces():
        ok, why = hand_over(exe, markup, summary, body)
        if ok:
            return True, "; ".join(tried)
        tried.append(why)
    return False, "; ".join(tried)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- the timer
#
# Derived from the cadence at install time rather than typed, so the unit and the program
# cannot disagree about which mornings are standup mornings -- and the program is still the
# authority, because a knob changed after an install changes the gate and only the gate.
# The timezone rides in the OnCalendar expression: the whole cadence is a wall clock in one
# city, and a unit that says 10:15 in whatever zone the laptop woke up in is the same
# silent hours-off bug arc-standup's `zone()` exists to avoid.

def oncalendar(cad, lead):
    days = ",".join(SYSTEMD_DAYS[d] for d in cad.get("days") or [])
    hh, mm = (cad.get("time") or "10:30").split(":")
    at = (datetime(2000, 1, 1, int(hh), int(mm)) - timedelta(minutes=lead)).strftime("%H:%M")
    tz = cad.get("tz") or ""
    return f"{days} {at}" + (f" {tz}" if tz else "")


def have_systemd_user():
    try:
        return subprocess.run(["systemctl", "--user", "show-environment"],
                              stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                              timeout=15).returncode == 0
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return False


def install_timer(cad, lead):
    tmpl = REPO / "systemd"
    for unit in (SERVICE_UNIT, TIMER_UNIT):
        if not (tmpl / unit).is_file():
            print(f"arc-standup-notify: no {unit} in {tmpl}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
    if not have_systemd_user():
        print("arc-standup-notify: no systemd user manager answering here.",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    when = oncalendar(cad, lead)
    UNIT_DIR.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    svc = (tmpl / SERVICE_UNIT).read_text()
    svc = re.sub(r"(?m)^ExecStart=.*$", f"ExecStart={Path(__file__).resolve()}", svc)
    (UNIT_DIR / SERVICE_UNIT).write_text(svc)
    tmr = (tmpl / TIMER_UNIT).read_text()
    tmr = re.sub(r"(?m)^OnCalendar=.*$", f"OnCalendar={when}", tmr)
    (UNIT_DIR / TIMER_UNIT).write_text(tmr)

    subprocess.run(["systemctl", "--user", "daemon-reload"], check=False)
    subprocess.run(["systemctl", "--user", "enable", "--now", TIMER_UNIT], check=False)
    print(f"installed: {UNIT_DIR / TIMER_UNIT} (OnCalendar={when}, Persistent=false)")
    print(f"           derived from STANDUP_DAYS/STANDUP_TIME/STANDUP_TZ minus {lead} min")
    subprocess.run(["systemctl", "--user", "list-timers", "--all", "--no-pager", TIMER_UNIT],
                   check=False)
    return 0


def uninstall_timer():
    if have_systemd_user():
        subprocess.run(["systemctl", "--user", "disable", "--now", TIMER_UNIT],
                       stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, check=False)
    for unit in (TIMER_UNIT, SERVICE_UNIT):
        try:
            (UNIT_DIR / unit).unlink()
        except FileNotFoundError:
            pass
    if have_systemd_user():
        subprocess.run(["systemctl", "--user", "daemon-reload"], check=False)
    print(f"removed {TIMER_UNIT} and {SERVICE_UNIT} from {UNIT_DIR}")
    return 0


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- entry

def main(argv=None):
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description="Deliver the standup prep shortly before the standup.",
        add_help=False)
    p.add_argument("-h", "--help", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--check", action="store_true",
                   help="say what it would do and why, and do nothing")
    p.add_argument("--print", dest="show", action="store_true",
                   help="print the prep it holds")
    p.add_argument("--force", action="store_true",
                   help="notify whatever the clock and the marker say")
    p.add_argument("--at", default="",
                   help="pretend it is this instant (ISO 8601)")
    p.add_argument("--install-timer", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--uninstall-timer", action="store_true")
    args = p.parse_args(argv)

    if args.help:
        sys.stdout.write(__doc__.lstrip("\n"))
        return 0

    load_env()
    lead = 15
    raw_lead = os.environ.get("STANDUP_NOTIFY_LEAD", "")
    if raw_lead:
        try:
            lead = int(raw_lead)
        except ValueError:
            log(f"could not read STANDUP_NOTIFY_LEAD={raw_lead!r} — using 15")
    if lead <= 0:
        log(f"STANDUP_NOTIFY_LEAD={lead} is not a lead time — using 15")
        lead = 15

    su = arc_standup()

    now = None
    if args.at:
        try:
            now = datetime.fromisoformat(args.at)
        except ValueError:
            print(f"arc-standup-notify: --at {args.at!r} is not an ISO 8601 instant",
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 2

    if args.uninstall_timer:
        return uninstall_timer()

    cad = su.cadence(now)
    if args.install_timer:
        return install_timer(cad, lead)

    now = cad["now"]
    st, missing_why = read_sidecar()

    if args.show:
        if st is None:
            print(f"arc-standup-notify: {missing_why}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 3
        sys.stdout.write(body_text(st) + "\n")
        return 0

    mode, why, overrode = decide(cad, st, missing_why, now, lead, force=args.force)
    # The force annotation is on the log line and never on the card. The log has to be able
    # to tell a delivery the clock asked for from one a person did; the person holding the
    # screen already knows which they typed.
    detail = why + (f" (--force past {', '.join(overrode)})" if overrode else "")

    # The same code the real run would exit with, so `--check && something` reads the way
    # it looks and a person comparing the two is not comparing two conventions.
    if args.check:
        print({"full": "would notify: ",
               "pointer": "would notify with a pointer: ",
               "silent": "would say nothing: "}[mode] + detail)
        return 3 if mode == "silent" else 0

    if mode == "silent":
        log(f"said nothing — {detail}")
        return 3

    if mode == "pointer":
        summary, body = pointer(cad, now, why)
    else:
        summary, body = summary_line(cad, now), body_text(st)

    ok, err = deliver(summary, body)
    if not ok:
        log(f"prep was ready and could not be delivered — {err}")
        return 1
    # Only when the first surface refused. A fallback that happens silently is a feature
    # quietly running in its degraded mode for a month.
    if err:
        log(f"fell back to a second notifier — {err}")

    # Not on --force, and this is not a detail: --force is a person asking to see it now,
    # and burning the morning's one automatic delivery to answer that would make looking at
    # the thing the reason it never arrived.
    #
    # The pointer spends it too. It is the morning's interruption, and having said "the
    # page did not build" once, saying it again at 10:16 is the nagging this refuses to do.
    if not args.force:
        write_marker((cad.get("for") or {}).get("iso"), now)

    if mode == "pointer":
        log(f"handed over a pointer instead of the prep — {detail}")
        return 0
    said = sum(len(b.get("items") or []) for b in st.get("beats") or [])
    log(f"delivered {said} line" + ("" if said == 1 else "s") + f" — {detail}")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
